#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134881} :activity/id #uuid "1129f5af-8b20-5759-9f3e-97b087634b1b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049353 :topic/id #uuid "ee26b28d-c213-5819-bf4f-97826f641d1d" :topic/math-academy-id 2148} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135013 :tutorial/content "**Periodic functions** are special functions that repeat themselves every ![](images/b3/b332ba26d1b8a3e4b8f817ed7eed0f807c5cc8457a0f29182ba04d25cbcb41b7.svg) units. The smallest positive value of ![](images/b3/b332ba26d1b8a3e4b8f817ed7eed0f807c5cc8457a0f29182ba04d25cbcb41b7.svg) that has this property is called the **period** of the function. \n\nLet's take the following periodic function as an example:\n\n![](images/c8/c8012ef5d26d04c81a6200bffb85babe8787c49258f5c014e6ac42ccbbbbf3cb.png)\n\nThis function repeats itself with a period of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) To illustrate, first focus on the part of the graph shown in blue below.\n\n![](images/c3/c309cafb184c76a6630f4357e07267ad832e85dd982fffce3abd28bf1526ec95.png)\n\nIf we shift this part ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to the right, the graph remains unchanged!\n\n![](images/a8/a82b5b1fd4da072d954d19bae0a790e82704343ec8b9d4448076f9daebe90617.png)\n\nThe same would happen if we shifted the part ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) units to the left. \n\nBy knowing the period of a periodic function, we can use a value of the function to infer many other values of the function. For example, in the function above, note that ![](images/4b/4ba0fb2ba36b90b8993c542124873b19eb240bed743d3c7bce2712f043187c4e.svg) Since the period is ![](images/0b/0be337b1b7891cff68331fbd1be3c8eb739eb8f47e7788e487ab91a8b2c88b67.svg) if we add (or subtract) any multiple of ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) from the input, the output stays the same. We can write this as\n\n![](images/b3/b3d30eb46010a29136217b3d004e0878a8923a94e77a52192172638697b6c67a.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/fb/fb2a8560bbc7122a4dd4f1a0bb9862d4368a63f4b03968884a3a60e7423a852c.svg) is an integer. \n\nFor example, setting ![](images/a4/a44568f6fc5aa5bd466f76f6220d308431c1187631cf36f8bebf2290ef7a0d83.svg) gives the following:\n\n![](images/1e/1e73284bd11385d40e11887964fa4ad1a7f523982c885c5fca7f87197ea1c954.svg)\n\nThe same is true for negative values of ![](images/c2/c2475d72e6ec36042292de6c0160930174172b2386148d823a70e59fddfb93c2.svg) Setting ![](images/55/55af3f37f9d82f0e1d9e6a77096eba247f13ea48ea005c2fd0b8719f1d6c04c1.svg) gives the following:\n\n![](images/7d/7df85879c234dbc914f4f4ee9261f4e12f0a57b03ae7ab433ddd0c8c4ba14d7f.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "b8020c2e-cfe4-5187-94df-23d7d0fcb605" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1502 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fa1d756d-c380-5339-a443-11ba085cc909" :step/math-academy-id 3664 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133469 :question/id #uuid "9a8dfc78-929c-525b-9403-7c1d11d8713f" :question/math-academy-id "e-1124"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ebe5738-7682-55ad-a21b-235063f08b88" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049534}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552518}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Period of a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "5c25e487-4990-59fc-bfc4-1a9c9ad30723" :step/math-academy-id 3665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133903 :knowledge-point/canonical-exampl